      "title": "Determining the Range of a Parametric Equation Defined in Terms of Trigonometric Functions"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "a418b355-7277-5045-913a-246f69c67c25",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta+1\n$$\n\n$$\ny=5\\sin\\theta-1\n$$\n\n$$\n0\\le \\theta<2\\pi\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y$?\n\nThe range of $x$ is:\n\n{{field-1}} {{field-2}} $x\\le$ {{field-3}}.\n\nThe range of $y$ is:\n\n{{field-4}} $\\le y$ {{field-5}} {{field-6}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Consider each coordinate separately.\n\nFor $x$:\n\nOver the full turn, $\\cos\\theta$ attains both $-1$ and $1$, including at permitted parameter values.\n\nMultiply by $2$ and translate by $1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-1 &\\le \\cos\\theta\\le 1 \\\\\n-2 &\\le 2\\cos\\theta\\le 2 \\\\\n-1 &\\le 2\\cos\\theta+1\\le 3 \\\\\n-1 &\\le x\\le 3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $-1\\le x\\le 3$.\n\nFor $y$:\n\nOver the full turn, $\\sin\\theta$ attains both $-1$ and $1$, including at permitted parameter values.\n\nMultiply by $5$ and translate by $-1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-1 &\\le \\sin\\theta\\le 1 \\\\\n-5 &\\le 5\\sin\\theta\\le 5 \\\\\n-6 &\\le 5\\sin\\theta-1\\le 4 \\\\\n-6 &\\le y\\le 4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $-6\\le y\\le 4$.\n\nContinuity gives every value between the stated bounds. The coordinate extrema need not occur at the same parameter value.",
